  <!-- Hidden guide summary contents (cloned into the modal on open) -->
  <div id="tcGuideContents" hidden data-pdf-base="{{ trainingVideos.guidePdfUrl }}">
    <section data-guide-content="install">
      <ol class="tc-guide-steps">
        <li><strong>Fit the antenna.</strong> Screw the antenna firmly onto the docking station.</li>
        <li><strong>Confirm the PC.</strong> The PC must run Windows — other operating systems are not supported. Low-power Pentium or Celeron processors are not supported.</li>
        <li><strong>Connect the dock.</strong> Use the supplied USB-C to USB-A cable to connect the dock directly to a free USB port on the PC.</li>
        <li><strong>Dock the sensor.</strong> Confirm the dock LED is blue, and that both the sensor and the dock appear in Windows Device Manager.</li>
        <li><strong>Launch &amp; check.</strong> Start TWAIN and check the battery status. If the status is not &ldquo;Ready&rdquo;, let the DC-Air charge for at least 1 hour, then continue.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="tc-guide-callout"><strong>Order matters.</strong> TWAIN is launched <em>after</em> the DC-Air has been docked, not before.</p>
      <p class="tc-guide-warn"><strong>Not supported:</strong> virtual connections, parallel machines, tablets, USB extensions / hubs, and cables longer than 2&nbsp;m. Connect the dock straight into a PC USB port only.</p>
    </section>

    <section data-guide-content="workflow">
      <h3 class="tc-guide-sub">Prepare &amp; position</h3>
      <ol class="tc-guide-steps">
        <li><strong>Undock and wait</strong> 3 seconds before using the sensor.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the LEDs.</strong> A double green flash on the sensor means BLE is connected; the dock LED must be steady blue.</li>
        <li><strong>New sheath</strong> — a fresh protective sheath for every patient.</li>
        <li><strong>Mount on the holder.</strong> Choose the correct holder, mount the DC-Air, attach the stick and alignment ring.</li>
        <li><strong>Position gently</strong> in the mouth.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3 class="tc-guide-sub">Expose &amp; finish</h3>
      <ol class="tc-guide-steps">
        <li><strong>Align the cone</strong> through the alignment ring until it almost touches the bite block, keeping the cone axis parallel — no cone-cut.</li>
        <li><strong>Expose &amp; hold out.</strong> Shoot, then hold the sensor outside the mouth until transfer is complete (about 6 seconds).</li>
        <li><strong>Reposition and repeat</strong> as needed.</li>
        <li><strong>Dock.</strong> Always dock the DC-Air after use and between patients.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="tc-guide-warn"><strong>Never</strong> re-use a sheath between patients, and never use the sensor without a sheath inside the patient&rsquo;s mouth.</p>
    </section>

    <section data-guide-content="battery">
      <h3 class="tc-guide-sub">LED quick reference</h3>
      <ul class="tc-guide-list">
        <li><strong>Sensor &middot; double green flash</strong> — BLE connected, ready to use (just undocked).</li>
        <li><strong>Sensor &middot; steady yellow</strong> — error, almost always a depleted or empty battery. Dock and charge before continuing.</li>
        <li><strong>Sensor &middot; off</strong> — asleep or switched off. Dock to wake / charge.</li>
        <li><strong>Dock &middot; steady blue</strong> — dock powered and charging.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 class="tc-guide-sub">Battery level in TWAIN</h3>
      <ul class="tc-guide-list">
        <li><strong>100&nbsp;%</strong> — fully charged. Normal use; dock between patients.</li>
        <li><strong>75&nbsp;%</strong> — normal working level. A drop from 100&nbsp;% to 75&nbsp;% after one exposure is normal.</li>
        <li><strong>50&nbsp;%</strong> — still ready for use; charge when convenient.</li>
        <li><strong>10&nbsp;%</strong> — battery low. Dock and charge before continuing.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="tc-guide-callout"><strong>Charging protocol:</strong> dock after every use and between patients. Leave the DC-Air docked overnight with the PC on, so the USB port stays powered.</p>
    </section>

    <section data-guide-content="ble">
      <h3 class="tc-guide-sub">Normal BLE behaviour</h3>
      <ul class="tc-guide-list">
        <li>Transmission begins about 1 second after the trigger and completes in about 5&ndash;7 seconds.</li>
        <li>Hold the sensor outside the mouth, in line of sight with the dock, during transfer.</li>
        <li>If BLE drops, the dock automatically retries three times within 30 seconds — give it that time before intervening.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 class="tc-guide-sub">Where to place the dock</h3>
      <ul class="tc-guide-list">
        <li><strong>Recommended:</strong> in front of the patient, or within &plusmn;45&deg;, with a clear line of sight to the sensor during transfer.</li>
        <li><strong>Avoid:</strong> behind the patient or head/neck, and keeping the sensor inside a closed mouth during transfer.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="tc-guide-callout"><strong>If BLE disconnects:</strong> screw the antenna on firmly &middot; dock and charge &ge;&nbsp;45 min if the battery is depleted &middot; reposition the dock in front / &plusmn;45&deg; &middot; wait for the 3 automatic reconnections; if it does not recover, restart TWAIN and reconnect the dock.</p>
    </section>

    <section data-guide-content="xram">
      <ul class="tc-guide-list">
        <li><strong>XRAM</strong> is onboard memory that safely keeps one image — the last one acquired. A new trigger replaces it; the image is lost only if the sensor switches off.</li>
        <li><strong>Image Pending</strong> is raised when an image was acquired but not transferred or displayed. It tells you there is an image in XRAM — so you do not retake the X-ray unnecessarily.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 class="tc-guide-sub">Use &ldquo;Download Last Image&rdquo; when&hellip;</h3>
      <ul class="tc-guide-list">
        <li>The Image Pending flag is active.</li>
        <li>You shot X-rays but did not receive an image on screen.</li>
        <li>You have just restarted frozen software — the image is still in XRAM.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="tc-guide-warn"><strong>Do not retake.</strong> If no image appears, check Image Pending / Download Last Image before retaking. Retaking wastes a frame and exposes the patient to additional dose unnecessarily.</p>
    </section>

    <section data-guide-content="qa">
      <p class="tc-guide-intro">The guide covers these problems one by one — each with causes, what to do, and when to contact support:</p>
      <ul class="tc-guide-list tc-guide-list-2col">
        <li><strong>A</strong> Noisy / grainy image</li>
        <li><strong>B</strong> Line or other artifacts</li>
        <li><strong>C</strong> BLE disconnected</li>
        <li><strong>D</strong> BLE connected but no image transmitted</li>
        <li><strong>E</strong> X-rays shot but no trigger</li>
        <li><strong>F</strong> Docked during BLE transmission</li>
        <li><strong>G</strong> Rapid docking / undocking</li>
        <li><strong>H</strong> Rogue or self triggers</li>
        <li><strong>I</strong> Front cover came off</li>
        <li><strong>J</strong> Consistent black or white images</li>
        <li><strong>K</strong> Dock not recognised in Device Manager</li>
        <li><strong>L</strong> Docking-station broken tabs</li>
        <li><strong>M</strong> Software / third-party imaging issues</li>
        <li><strong>N</strong> Data corruption / lost synchronisation</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="tc-guide-callout">A precise <strong>date and time</strong> of the event is the single most useful thing you can provide to support.</p>
    </section>
  </div>

